\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Which moves survive a quotient?}

\p{A quotient of observations is useful only when the moves still have a
well-defined action on the observations. Let #{X} be a declared sticker-state
set, let #{G} act on #{X}, and let the surjection #{q:X\twoheadrightarrow Y}
identify the observations we want to retain (equivalently, take #{Y=q(X)}).
The move #{g\in G} \newvocab{preserves the fibers} of #{q} when}
##{q(x)=q(x')\Longrightarrow q(gx)=q(gx')\qquad(x,x'\in X).}
\p{Exactly in this case there is a unique induced map #{\bar g:Y\to Y} with}
##{q\circ g=\bar g\circ q.}
\p{If #{g} is a bijection and #{X} is finite, then #{\bar g} is surjective and
therefore bijective. In an infinite setting, require the inverse move to
preserve fibers as well. Thus, for the finite cube action below, the
fiber-preserving bijections form a subgroup; a quotient does not
automatically inherit the whole puzzle action.}

\subtree{
  \title{The antipodal sticker quotient}

  \p{For the ordinary cube, use the surface-cell and frame convention in
  [how the cube models connect](frap-001A) and [the complete three-layer
  cube](frap-0016). Represent a sticker by a pair #{(p,n)}, where
  #{p\in\{-1,0,1\}^3} satisfies #{\lVert p\rVert_\infty=1} and
  #{n=\operatorname{sgn}(p_i)e_i} for each nonzero coordinate #{p_i}.
  Thus corners, edges and face centers contribute #{8\cdot3}, #{12\cdot2}
  and #{6\cdot1} pairs, respectively, for 54 stickers. The antipodal
  identification is the involution}
  ##{a(p,n)=(-p,-n),\qquad q(p,n)=\{(p,n),a(p,n)\}.}
  \p{Its fibers pair opposite stickers. This is a quotient of the labelled
  sticker action; it is not the same as forgetting equal colors or quotienting
  by whole-cube rotations.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Why one face turn fails}

  \p{The positive-#{z} face turn uses}
  ##{u(x,y,z)=(y,-x,z)}
  \p{on every sticker whose cubie has #{z=1}, rotating both its position and
  its normal, and fixes the other cubies. Take a top sticker and its antipode,
  for example}
  ##{x=((1,1,1),e_z),\qquad a(x)=((-1,-1,-1),-e_z).}
  \p{The turn sends #{x} to the sticker at
  #{((1,-1,1),e_z)}, while it fixes #{a(x)}. These outputs are not an
  antipodal pair, so #{q(ux)\ne q(u\,a(x))}. The single #{U} move therefore
does not descend to the antipodal quotient.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{A synchronized opposite-layer move}

  \p{The clockwise turn of the negative-#{z} face, viewed from that face, is
the inverse global rotation on #{z=-1}. Write it as #{d}; its inverse #{d^{-1}}
uses the same global rotation as #{u}. The composite #{u d^{-1}} rotates both
opposite layers by the same linear map and fixes the middle cubies. It commutes
with the antipodal involution:}
  ##{a\,(u d^{-1})=(u d^{-1})\,a.}
  \p{Consequently it preserves every antipodal fiber and induces a bijection
on #{Y}. A direct check of all 54 stickers gives the same result: #{U} fails
on the displayed pair, while the synchronized #{U D'} move maps every
antipodal pair to an antipodal pair. The surviving action is therefore an
action of the fiber-preserving subgroup, not of the six ordinary face turns
individually.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{What the cover does not provide}

  \p{A covering or quotient of the underlying shape supplies a map of shape
  data. It does not by itself supply a map between sticker-state spaces, a
  chosen fiber relation, or an equivariance proof for each legal move. Those
  data are separate: the explicit cube action records which stickers a face
  turn moves, while the quotient records which states are identified. A move
  descends only after the displayed fiber test has been checked.}

  \p{This is the precise limit of the comparison with the complete unfolding
  in [label transport and topology](frap-0014). Projectivity and branched
  unfolding describe transport of simplex labels; the antipodal example
  describes descent of a particular sticker action. They pose analogous
  questions about information lost by identification, but neither construction
  is being identified with the other. A general lifting or descent
  classification is outside this note.}
}

\p{The practical rule is short: state the state space, the quotient map and
the move action before saying that a move survives identification. The
antipodal calculation supplies both a negative and a positive finite example,
while preserving the distinction between a shape cover and a compatible
puzzle action.}
